<p>Mandatory status depends on the current Quality Control Order or CRS notification, and dates are often extended. Check the latest position in our <a href="/resources/standards/mandatory-bis-standard">mandatory BIS standards library</a> and <a href="/resources/standards/mandatory-crs-standards">mandatory CRS standards list</a>.</p>

<h2>Which Mark Is Necessary on Electrical Appliances?</h2>
<p>It depends on whether the product is an <strong>electrical appliance</strong> or an <strong>electronic or IT product</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Household electrical appliances</strong> such as irons, kettles, mixers, toasters, water heaters and room heaters need the <strong>ISI mark</strong> under the IS 302 series, as notified in the appliance Quality Control Order.</li>
<li><strong>Electronic and IT goods</strong> such as adaptors, TVs, laptops, set-top boxes and speakers need the <strong>Standard Mark with an R-number</strong> under the BIS Compulsory Registration Scheme.</li>
<li><strong>Energy-using appliances</strong> such as ACs, refrigerators, fans, water heaters and TVs may also need a <strong>BEE star label</strong>. This shows efficiency, not safety.</li>
</ul>
<p>So in an exam the expected answer is usually <strong>"ISI mark"</strong>. In practice, check the exact product, because many items now sit under CRS. Our <a href="/resources/blogs/bis-compulsory-registration-scheme-crs-explained">CRS guide</a> explains that route.</p>

<h2>Which Safety Mark Is Standard on Helmets?</h2>
<p>The <strong>ISI mark</strong>. Protective helmets for riders of two-wheelers must conform to <strong>IS 4151</strong> and carry the ISI mark with a valid licence number. Selling or making non-ISI two-wheeler helmets is not allowed. Look for the CM/L number under the mark and verify it in the BIS Care app.</p>

<h2>Which Standard Applies to Ceiling Fans?</h2>
<p><strong>IS 374</strong> covers electric ceiling type fans, and they need the <strong>ISI mark</strong>. Ceiling fans are also covered by BEE's star labelling programme, so a typical fan box carries both the ISI mark and a BEE star label.</p>

<h2>Is the ISI Mark Required on Water Bottles?</h2>
<p>It depends on what "water bottle" means:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Packaged drinking water</strong> sold in sealed bottles or jars no longer has to carry the ISI mark under <strong>IS 14543</strong>. FSSAI removed the requirement on 17 October 2024; the plant needs an FSSAI licence, and the ISI mark is now voluntary.</li>
<li><strong>Packaged natural mineral water</strong> under <strong>IS 13428</strong> is in the same position: an FSSAI licence is required and the ISI mark is voluntary.</li>
<li><strong>An empty reusable bottle</strong> is a different product. Whether it needs a mark depends on its material and type and on any current Quality Control Order, so check before you import or sell.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Other Marks You May See</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>BIS Hallmark:</strong> for gold and silver jewellery, with a 6-digit HUID code.</li>
<li><strong>FSSAI logo:</strong> for food businesses and packaged food.</li>
<li><strong>AGMARK:</strong> for graded agricultural produce, mostly voluntary.</li>
<li><strong>Ecomark:</strong> a voluntary BIS label for environment-friendly products.</li>
<li><strong>Legal Metrology declarations:</strong> name, address, net quantity and MRP on packaged goods. These are not a mark but are mandatory.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How to Check Any Product Yourself</h2>
<ol>
<li>Find the product in our standards library or the BIS list of products under compulsory certification.</li>
<li>Note the IS number and whether it is under the ISI scheme or CRS.</li>
<li>Check the date from which it is mandatory and any extensions.</li>
<li>On the product, verify the licence or registration number in the BIS Care app or the CRS portal.</li>
</ol>

